;; FileSet























(go/type FileSet "A FileSet represents a set of source files.\nMethods of file sets are synchronized; multiple goroutines\nmay invoke them concurrently.\n\nThe byte offsets for each file in a file set are mapped into\ndistinct (integer) intervals, one interval [base, base+size]\nper file. [FileSet.Base] represents the first byte in the file, and size\nis the corresponding file size. A [Pos] value is a value in such\nan interval. By determining the interval a [Pos] value belongs\nto, the file, its file base, and thus the byte offset (position)\nthe [Pos] value is representing can be computed.\n\nWhen adding a new file, a file base must be provided. That can\nbe any integer value that is past the end of any interval of any\nfile already in the file set. For convenience, [FileSet.Base] provides\nsuch a value, which is simply the end of the Pos interval of the most\nrecently added file, plus one. Unless there is a need to extend an\ninterval later, using the [FileSet.Base] should be used as argument\nfor [FileSet.AddFile].\n\nA [File] may be removed from a FileSet when it is no longer needed.\nThis may reduce memory usage in a long-running application.\n" (struct
    ^{:line 426 :tag sync/RWMutex} mutex ; protects the file set
    ^{:line 427 :tag int} base ; base offset for the next file
    ^{:line 428 :tag tree} tree ; tree of files in ascending base order
    ^{:line 429 :tag (atomic/Pointer File)} last)) ; cache of last file looked up



